.TH "lts.conf" "5" "2026-09-17" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
lts.conf \(em Per\-client configuration for LTSP clients
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fIlts.conf\fR configures LTSP clients. It is read on the client at boot by
getltscfg(1), and directives apply from the most specific matching section
downwards: a section named by MAC address, IP or hostname overrides
\fB[Default]\fR.
.PP
This page aims to be complete: every directive the client actually reads is
listed here. Where a value is derived rather than configured, that is said so,
because the derived ones are the ones that surprise people.
.PP
Boolean directives accept \fBTrue\fR, \fBYes\fR, \fBY\fR and \fB1\fR, in any
mix of case, and anything else counts as false. A directive that takes a
numbered series \(em \fBSCREEN_\fP\fInn\fP, \fBFSTAB_\fP\fInn\fP and the rest
\(em is read in sorted order, so the numbers decide the order of the resulting
lines, not the order they appear in the file.
.SH "SESSION BACKEND"
.PP
.IP "\fBLDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND\fP" 4
Which backend ldm uses to open the session. \fBxfreerdp\fR opens a remote
desktop against an xrdp server; \fBssh\fR is the original LTSP behaviour and
remains the built\-in default.
.IP "" 4
Precedence, highest first: \fBLDM_FORCE_BACKEND\fR, then whatever the user
picks in the greeter, then \fBLDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND\fR, then \fBssh\fR. Set
\fBLDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND\fR to choose the usual case while leaving the greeter
menu available; set \fBLDM_FORCE_BACKEND\fR to take the choice away.
.IP "" 4
Example: \fBLDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND="xfreerdp"\fR
.IP "\fBLDM_FORCE_BACKEND\fP" 4
Same values, but the greeter offers no alternative.
.IP "\fBRDP_SERVER\fP" 4
Host the \fBxfreerdp\fR backend connects to. Point it at a load balancer
virtual address rather than at one session server, so that a failed server
moves its users without anyone editing a client.
.IP "\fBRDP_OPTIONS\fP" 4
Passed through to xfreerdp verbatim. \fB/dynamic\-resolution\fR follows the
client window size, \fB/sound\fR and \fB/clipboard\fR redirect those channels,
\fB/cert:ignore\fR accepts the server certificate unconditionally \(em
acceptable on a closed segment, a decision worth revisiting elsewhere.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH\fP" 4
Check the credentials \fIbefore\fR starting the RDP client. Off by default;
\fBFalse\fR or unset means no check at all, as before this existed.
.IP "" 4
\fBTrue\fR means "check", and lets the client pick the method. A configured
realm \(em \fBRDP_PREAUTH_REALM\fR, or \fBdefault_realm\fR in krb5.conf \(em
says there is a domain worth asking, so Kerberos is used. Without one, the
ssh probe is the only thing that can answer, so that is used instead. One
spelling therefore works in both kinds of deployment.
.IP "" 4
\fBkrb5\fR and \fBssh\fR force one method outright, for sites that would
rather be told their chosen method is not set up than have another substituted.
.IP "" 4
The automatic fallback to ssh happens only when the host keys are actually
present; the explicit \fBssh\fR is not gated that way. The reason is that the
ssh probe refuses on an unverified host key and does not fail open there, so
on a client with no \fIssh_known_hosts\fR it would reject every login \(em and
arriving at that by upgrading, rather than by asking for it, would be a
lockout nobody chose.
.IP "" 4
Worth understanding before enabling. xrdp has no server\-side NLA, so it
accepts the connection whatever the password is and validates inside the
session, in its own dialog. A wrong password therefore does not end the
connection: ldm never regains control and cannot tell the user what went
wrong, and the connection stays open \(em measured against xrdp 0.10.1, a full
minute after AUTHFAIL. With this set, ldm asks first and reports the real
reason in the greeter. Kerberos distinguishes the most: wrong password, locked
account, expired password, clock skew too large, KDC unreachable. sshd is
deliberately vaguer - it answers "Permission denied" to a wrong password, a
disabled account and a denied service alike, which is a security property
rather than an oversight.
.IP "" 4
It fails open. Any configuration problem \(em no realm, no krb5.conf \(em lets
the connection proceed exactly as before, rather than locking people out of a
working server because a check could not run.
.IP "" 4
It does not make the client a domain member: there is no machine account and
no keytab, only the credentials the user has just typed, and the ticket is
discarded as soon as it arrives.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_REALM\fP" 4
Realm for the Kerberos variant. Defaults to the system default realm from
krb5.conf.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH=ssh\fP" 4
Asks sshd on the session host whether it accepts the credentials. That tests
more than the password: it runs the PAM stack of the machine which will host
the session, so an expired account, a \fBpam_access\fR rule or an sssd access
rule deny here too - the kind of refusal a KDC knows nothing about.
.IP "" 4
It costs something Kerberos does not. Kerberos never puts the password on the
wire; this sends it to whatever answers on that address. A thin client boots a
read-only image and so has no persistent \fIknown_hosts\fR - trust on first
use never gets past the first use, because every boot is a first use. The host
keys therefore have to be known in advance:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ltsp\-update\-sshkeys \-e /etc/ltsp/ssh_known_hosts.app 172.17.250.31
.fi
.IP "" 4
The next image build picks that up, into the image's own
\fIssh_known_hosts\fR. What it copies is \fBssh_host_*_key.pub\fR \(em public
keys; nothing
secret goes into the image. With an unknown or changed key, ssh refuses
\fIbefore\fR asking for a password, so nothing is disclosed. That case is
deliberately the one place this check does not fail open.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_HOST\fP" 4
Host to ask, for the ssh variant. Optional: it defaults to \fBRDP_SERVER\fR
and is only needed when \fBRDP_SERVER\fR is a load balancer. HAProxy forwards
3389, so an ssh connection to that address reaches the balancer rather than a
session server, where the accounts may not exist at all. Either name a session
server here, or give the balancer a second listener on port 22 with the same
stick\-table, so the check lands on the server that is about to get the
session.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS\fP" 4
Where to look for the host keys. Defaults to \fI/etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts\fR,
which is where ltsp\-update\-sshkeys(8) puts them.
.IP "" 4
With no entry for the host it would ask, the check is skipped and says so in
the log, naming the host and the file; the login proceeds exactly as it would
with \fBRDP_PREAUTH\fR unset. Missing configuration is a gap, not an attack,
and refusing every login over it would be a lockout \(em announced, on top of
that, as "the session server could not be verified", which points at the wrong
problem. A key that is present and does \fInot\fR match still refuses: that is
the case the check exists to catch.
.IP "" 4
The test is per host, not per file, and the difference bites. An image carries
whatever \fIssh_known_hosts\fR was baked into it at build time, and those keys
belong to the site it was built for. Deployed anywhere else the file is
present, non\-empty and irrelevant \(em a set of foreign keys used to be worse
than no keys at all.
.IP "\fBRDP_DOMAIN\fP" 4
Domains to offer in the greeter, separated by \fB|\fR. Setting it adds a domain
chooser; leaving it unset means the RDP client connects without one.
.IP "\fBRDP_DEFAULT_DOMAIN\fP" 4
The domain sent when the greeter offers no choice. Unset means none.
.IP "\fBRDP_XFREERDP_BIN\fP" 4
The FreeRDP binary to run. Normally left alone: the backend looks for
\fBxfreerdp3\fR and falls back to \fBxfreerdp\fR, which is right on every
current image. Set it to pin a self\-built client.
.IP "\fBLDM_SSHOPTIONS\fP" 4
Extra options for the ssh backend's connection, passed through to
\fBssh\fR(1) verbatim.
.IP "\fBSSH_OVERRIDE_PORT\fP" 4
The port the ssh backend connects to, when the session servers do not listen
on 22.
.IP "\fBLDM_AUTOLOGIN\fP" 4
Log in without asking, using \fBLDM_USERNAME\fR and \fBLDM_PASSWORD\fR. The
greeter never appears. Whoever can read lts.conf can then read the password,
and lts.conf travels over TFTP \(em see FILES.
.IP "\fBLDM_USERNAME\fP, \fBLDM_PASSWORD\fP" 4
The credentials for \fBLDM_AUTOLOGIN\fR, and the prefilled user name for a
kiosk\-style client.
.IP "\fBLDM_PASSWORD_HASH\fP" 4
Whether \fBLDM_PASSWORD\fR holds a hash rather than the password itself. It
keeps the plaintext out of the file; it does not keep anyone out of the
session, since the hash is what gets sent.
.IP "\fBHOST_TO_USERNAME\fP" 4
Derives \fBLDM_USERNAME\fR from the client's own name, as a
\fBsed\fR(1) substitution applied to it \(em \fB"s/ltsp/user/"\fR turns
\fBltsp20\fR into \fBuser20\fR. Where \fBLDM_PASSWORD\fR is unset it is
set to the same string, so the password is the user name: a lab where the
seat is the identity and there is nothing to protect. Say that out loud
before using it.
.IP "\fBLDM_GUESTLOGIN\fP" 4
Whether the greeter offers a guest button.
.IP "\fBLDM_AUTOLOGIN_SERVER\fP, \fBLDM_GUEST_SERVER\fP" 4
Which servers the automatic and guest logins go to, when they should not
follow the normal server list.
.IP "\fBLDM_SERVER\fP" 4
The session server, or several separated by spaces. Unset, the client asks the
server it booted from. With more than one, the greeter offers the list and
ldminfod(8) on each is asked which is least loaded.
.IP "\fBLDM_SESSION\fP, \fBLDM_XSESSION\fP, \fBLDM_DEFAULT_XSESSION\fP" 4
The session command to run after authentication. \fBLDM_SESSION\fR replaces it
outright; \fBLDM_XSESSION\fR names the wrapper script (normally
\fI/etc/X11/Xsession\fR); \fBLDM_DEFAULT_XSESSION\fR is the fallback when the
first two are unset.
.IP "\fBLDM_FORCE_SESSION\fP" 4
Removes the session chooser from the greeter, so the configured session is the
only one.
.IP "\fBLDM_LANGUAGE\fP, \fBLDM_FORCE_LANGUAGE\fP" 4
The preselected language, and whether to remove the language chooser along
with the choice.
.IP "\fBLDM_LOGIN_TIMEOUT\fP" 4
Seconds the greeter waits with nothing typed before logging in automatically.
Only useful together with a configured user; without one the timeout has
nothing to do.
.IP "\fBLDM_THEME\fP" 4
The greeter's theme directory under \fI/usr/share/ldm/themes\fR.
.IP "\fBLDM_GREETER\fP" 4
The greeter binary ldm starts. Replacing it is how a site writes its own.
.IP "\fBLDM_WINMANAGER\fP" 4
A window manager to run behind the greeter. Normally unnecessary: the greeter
is the only window on that display.
.IP "\fBLDM_CLOCK_FORMAT\fP" 4
See CLIENT IDENTITY.
.IP "\fBLDM_DIRECTX\fP" 4
Whether the session's X traffic bypasses the ssh tunnel. Faster, and
unencrypted on the wire \(em the password still goes through ssh, everything
afterwards does not.
.IP "\fBLDM_NOMAD\fP" 4
Enables the Nomad remote\-desktop mode, for the installations that use it.
.IP "\fBLDM_SYSLOG\fP, \fBLDM_LOGLEVEL\fP" 4
Whether ldm logs to syslog rather than \fI/var/log/ldm.log\fR, and how much.
The level runs from 0 to 7; 7 includes the pre\-authentication's own account of
what it asked and what it heard back, which is the setting to reach for when a
login fails without saying why.
.IP "" 4
On a thin client \fI/var/log\fR lives in RAM, so the log dies with the session
that produced it. \fBLDM_SYSLOG=True\fR together with \fBSYSLOG_HOST\fR puts
it on a machine that will still be running afterwards.
.SH "LOCKOUT WARNINGS"
.PP
Sites protect RDP with fail2ban, so a few rejected passwords block the client
at the network level for some minutes. From the seat that is baffling: the
screen that worked a moment ago now fails in an entirely different way, and
nothing can be learned by asking the server, because being unable to reach the
server is the state in question. These settings let the greeter warn before it
happens and say how long it lasts once it has.
.PP
What can honestly be counted was measured, not assumed. Against xrdp a
rejected password makes the RDP client neither exit nor report anything \(em
the session stays open showing xrdp's own login window, and the retries happen
in there, invisible to ldm. Failed RDP logins are therefore not countable from
the client at all. The pre\-authentication above is the one place a rejection
is visible, so it is where the counting happens, and these settings do nothing
unless \fBRDP_PREAUTH\fR is on.
.PP
That is not the limitation it appears to be, because pre\-authentication is
also the fix: a password rejected before the RDP client starts never reaches
the RDP server, so an RDP jail never sees it. What the count is for is the
lockout that happens \fIinstead\fR. \fBRDP_PREAUTH=ssh\fR leaves a failed
password in the sshd log, where an sshd jail bans the client \(em with
\fBbanaction_allports\fR, on every port, RDP included. \fBRDP_PREAUTH=krb5\fR
raises \fBbadPwdCount\fR on the domain controller, where the account lockout
policy is counting. Both punish the next attempt.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_MAXFAIL\fP" 4
How many rejections make the client count itself as blocked. Unset or 0 turns
the warnings off, which is the default: the right number is whatever the site
configured on the server, and a warning about a lockout that does not exist is
worse than silence. Set it to the jail's \fBmaxretry\fR, or to the domain's
lockout threshold.
.IP "" 4
At one below the number the greeter adds a warning to the rejection message;
at the number it stops sending attempts altogether and shows the remaining
wait, because another attempt would only restart the server's clock as well as
its own.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_FAILWINDOW\fP" 4
Seconds over which rejections are counted. Default 600. Match the jail's
\fBfindtime\fR.
.IP "\fBRDP_PREAUTH_LOCKTIME\fP" 4
How long the block lasts, in seconds. Default 600. Match the jail's
\fBbantime\fR. Only used for what the greeter says \(em the block itself is
enforced elsewhere, and this number does not shorten it.
.IP "" 4
A jail set to three strikes and three minutes:
.IP "" 4
.nf
RDP_PREAUTH=True
RDP_PREAUTH_MAXFAIL=3
RDP_PREAUTH_FAILWINDOW=180
RDP_PREAUTH_LOCKTIME=180
.fi
.PP
Two properties are worth knowing. The tally is a file under
\fI/run/ldm/lockout\fR, so it is shared by every screen on the client:
SCREEN_05 through SCREEN_08 are four greeters behind one address, and whatever
is doing the banning counts per address \(em a per\-screen count would see a
quarter of what the server sees, and somebody would be locked out without ever
having typed three wrong passwords at one screen. And \fI/run\fR is tmpfs, so
a reboot forgets the tally while the block outlives it; the greeter then says
nothing rather than guessing.
.PP
One caveat for \fBRDP_PREAUTH=ssh\fR behind a load balancer: see
\fBRDP_PREAUTH_HOST\fR. If the probe reaches the balancer rather than a
session server, the failed passwords are logged against the balancer, and a
jail there would ban it for everyone at once.
.SH "SCREENS"
.PP
.IP "\fBSCREEN_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
What to run on virtual terminal \fInn\fP \(em \fBldm\fR for the greeter,
\fBshell\fR for a login shell. Left unset, ltsp_config.d/15default-screen
picks \fBSCREEN_07=ldm\fR when ldm is installed.
.IP "\fBSCREEN_\fP\fInn\fP\fB_WAIT_FGCONSOLE\fP" 4
Whether screen_session waits for that terminal to become the foreground
console before starting anything on it. Defaults to true, which is right for
a terminal a getty has already brought to the front, and wrong for the one
ldm.service starts: under systemd nothing switches to tty7 beforehand, and
Xorg \(em which does switch, with its \fBvt7\fR argument \(em only starts
after the wait. The client then sits there forever.
.IP "" 4
Since 6.3.1 this is filled in as False for any \fBSCREEN_\fP\fInn\fP set to
\fBldm\fR, whoever wrote it, so it normally needs no attention. Set it
explicitly to keep control on a setup that really does switch terminals by
hand.
.IP "" 4
Worth knowing for older images, because the failure is silent and looks like
health: the client shows a console login, \fBldm.service\fR reports "active
(running)", nothing has failed, graphical.target is reached, and there is no
Xorg log and no ldm log to read. The tell is \fBscreen_session\fR with a
\fBsleep 2\fR child; \fBchvt 7\fR on the client confirms it in one command.
.IP "" 4
On a Debian client the greeter is started by \fBldm.service\fR, which runs
\fBscreen_session 07\fR for that default. \fBltsp\-screen@.service\fR is
shipped but deliberately not enabled: enable an instance only for an
\fIadditional\fR screen, and never one that collides with the VT7 ldm uses
\(em two greeters then fight over which terminal owns the session.
.IP "\fBSCREEN_DEFAULT\fP" 4
Which terminal is brought to the front once the client is up. Unset, the
highest\-numbered configured screen wins. Naming a terminal with no
\fBSCREEN_\fP\fInn\fP of its own is ignored, with a complaint on the console.
.PP
The scripts a screen can run live in \fI/usr/share/ltsp/screen.d\fR. Each takes
its own directives:
.IP "\fBldm\fR" 4
The graphical greeter. Everything under SESSION BACKEND applies.
.IP "\fBshell\fR" 4
A root shell on that terminal. Useful while building an image, and an open
door on a production one.
.IP "\fBssh\fR" 4
A text login over ssh. \fBSSH_HOST\fR is the host to reach, \fBserver\fR by
default; the user name is typed at the prompt.
.IP "\fBtelnet\fR" 4
A text login over telnet, to \fBTELNET_HOST\fR or the boot server. Sends the
password in clear.
.IP "\fBxdmcp\fR" 4
An X server querying a display manager on \fBXDM_SERVER\fR (the boot server by
default). \fBDISABLE_ACCESS_CONTROL\fR adds \fB\-ac\fR, which turns off X
access control for every client on the network, not only this one.
\fBstartx\fR is an alias for this screen.
.IP "\fBxterm\fR" 4
A bare X server with a single terminal window.
.IP "\fBkiosk\fR" 4
A browser, full screen, with nothing else reachable. \fBKIOSK_EXE\fR is the
program \(em Firefox, Chromium, Chrome or Opera, whichever is found first when
it is unset \(em and \fBKIOSK_OPTIONS\fR its arguments, usually the URL.
\fBKIOSKUSER\fR is the account it runs as (\fBltspkiosk\fR unless set,
created on the spot if it does not exist), \fBKIOSK_WM\fR a window manager to
put behind it, \fBKIOSK_DAEMON\fR whether to restart it when it exits, and
\fBKIOSK_USER_STARTUP\fR a command to run first.
.IP "\fBmenu\fR" 4
A text menu offering several of the above. \fBMENU_TITLE\fR is the prompt,
\fBMENU_ITEM_\fP\fInn\fP the label of each entry and \fBMENU_COMMAND_\fP\fInn\fP
what it runs, up to 20 of them. \fBMENU_LISTHEIGHT\fR sets the visible rows and
\fBMENU_APPEND_PATH\fR where the commands are looked for \(em \fBnone\fR means
the commands must carry their own path.
.IP "\fBxfreerdp\fR, \fBrdesktop\fR" 4
A remote desktop without a greeter in front of it. The RDP directives under
SESSION BACKEND apply.
.PP
Directives for the X screens themselves:
.IP "\fBX_ARGS\fP" 4
Extra arguments for the X server on a screen that starts one.
.IP "\fBXINITRC_DAEMON\fP" 4
Whether the session is restarted when it exits, rather than the screen ending.
.IP "\fBXINITRC_PROMPT_ON_EXIT\fP, \fBXINITRC_COMMAND_ON_EXIT\fP" 4
Whether to ask before the screen goes away, and what to run at that point.
.IP "\fBXINITRC_PROMPT_ON_ERROR\fP, \fBXINITRC_COMMAND_ON_ERROR\fP, \fBXINITRC_ERROR_CODES\fP" 4
The same for a session that ended badly: the exit codes that count as an
error, whether to say so, and what to run. Without a command of its own a
dialog appears saying only that an error occurred.
.IP "\fBLTS_OPTION1\fP, \fBLTS_OPTION2\fP" 4
Two free arguments appended to the session command, for a screen script that
wants them.
.IP "\fBUSE_XFS\fP, \fBXFS_SERVER\fP" 4
Whether to fetch fonts from a font server, and which. A relic: current X
clients carry their own fonts.
.SH "SSH HOST KEYS"
.PP
.IP "\fBSSH_KNOWN_HOSTS\fP, \fBSSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_\fP\fIn\fP" 4
Host keys to add to \fI/etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts\fR when the client boots.
Each value is one complete known_hosts line \(em host, key type, key \(em and
the numbered form takes as many as needed:
.IP "" 4
.nf
SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_0="172.17.250.31 ssh\-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza..."
SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_1="172.17.250.32 ssh\-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza..."
.fi
.IP "" 4
Where to get those lines. On any machine that can reach the session server:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ssh\-keyscan \-t ed25519 172.17.250.31
.fi
.IP "" 4
prints exactly one line in exactly the form this setting wants \(em host,
key type, key \(em and that line is what goes in the quotes. A server
without an ed25519 key answers to \fBssh\-keyscan\fR without \fB\-t\fR,
which lists every key it has; any one of them will do. The same thing read
from the server itself is
.IP "" 4
.nf
cat /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub
.fi
.IP "" 4
except that this file has no host name in front and a comment
(\fBroot@hostname\fR) at the end: prepend the address the client will use
and drop the comment. Verify it out of band \(em comparing
\fBssh\-keygen \-lf\fR on the server with what ssh\-keyscan returned is
the point of pinning a key at all.
.IP "" 4
Numbering starts at 0 and stops at the first gap, so a mistyped index shows up
as one missing key rather than silently dropping every key after it. Entries
are appended, so keys baked into the image by ltsp\-update\-sshkeys(8) are
kept; a line that is already present is not added twice, and a malformed line
or an unrecognised key type is refused with a message on the boot console
rather than written \(em ssh stops reading known_hosts at the first line it
cannot parse, which would quietly discard everything below it.
.IP "" 4
This exists for \fBRDP_PREAUTH=ssh\fR, which refuses to proceed against a host
key it does not recognise. A thin client boots a read\-only image and keeps no
known_hosts between boots, so trust on first use never gets past the first
use \(em every boot is one. Naming the keys here rather than baking them in
means a session server can be added, or its key rotated, without rebuilding
the image, and per\-client sections can give a pilot group a different set of
servers to trust than production has.
.IP "" 4
What it is worth: lts.conf is fetched over TFTP at boot (see FILES), which is
unauthenticated, so a key named here is only as trustworthy as that transfer.
That is not a step down from baking it into the image \(em the client already
fetches its kernel, initrd and whole root filesystem the same way, and
whoever can interfere with that owns the client regardless. The pinning is
exactly as strong as the boot chain and no stronger. It would become the weak
link the moment the boot chain is secured and lts.conf is left on plain TFTP.
.SH "PER-SCREEN DIRECTIVES"
.PP
Any directive can be given per screen by prefixing it with \fBSCREEN_\fInn\fB_\fR.
When \fBscreen_session\fR runs for screen \fInn\fR it renames those into the
plain names for that process only, so several greeters can run at once with
different settings:
.IP "" 4
.nf
SCREEN_05=ldm
SCREEN_06=ldm
SCREEN_05_RDP_SERVER=172.17.250.31
SCREEN_06_RDP_SERVER=172.17.249.223
SCREEN_06_RDP_PREAUTH=ssh
.fi
.IP "" 4
Each screen gets its own X server and its own DISPLAY, derived from the tty,
so they do not collide. Only the screen ldm.service starts (07 by default) is
enabled out of the box; the others are enabled by hand with
\fBsystemctl enable ltsp\-screen@\fP\fIn\fP. Note that every screen waits for
its virtual terminal to come to the foreground before starting unless
\fBSCREEN_\fInn\fB_WAIT_FGCONSOLE\fR is False \(em with it False on all of
them, all their X servers start at once, which costs the memory of each.
.SH "KEYBOARD"
.PP
.IP "\fBXKBLAYOUT\fP, \fBCONSOLE_KEYMAP\fP" 4
Set both, and set them before anyone tries to log in. With the client on a US
layout and the user on a German keyboard, every password containing a
character that moves between the layouts arrives wrong \(em a \fB\-\fR typed on
QWERTZ reaches the server as \fB/\fR. The domain rejects it as a wrong password
with nothing to suggest the layout is at fault, and the same credentials work
when typed on the server. The Kerberos log is what gives it away:
\fBPreauthentication failed\fR means wrong password, not unknown user and not
unreachable server.
.IP "\fBXKBMODEL\fP, \fBXKBVARIANT\fP, \fBXKBOPTIONS\fP, \fBXKBRULES\fP" 4
The rest of the X keyboard description, with the same meanings they have in
\fBxkeyboard\-config\fR(7): the physical model, the layout variant (\fBnodeadkeys\fR
and the like), extra options such as \fBcompose:menu\fR, and the rule set.
\fBXKBLAYOUT\fR alone is enough for most fleets.
.IP "\fBCONSOLE_FONT\fP" 4
The font loaded on the text console before systemd starts, as a name from
\fI/usr/share/consolefonts\fR with or without the \fI.psf.gz\fR \(em for
example \fBUni3\-Terminus16\fR. Unset, the client runs the font console\-setup
already cached in the image, which is the right answer almost always.
.IP "" 4
It exists because of what the early boot looks like without it. The kernel's
built\-in font maps Latin\-1 and nothing else, while systemd writes its messages
in the client's locale, quotes unit names typographically \(em \fB\(dqfoo\(dq\fR
on a German system, \fB'foo'\fR on an English one \(em and ends them with an
ellipsis. None of those characters are in the built\-in font, so every one of
them is drawn as an empty box until console\-setup runs, which its service does
at \fBmulti\-user.target\fR: long after the messages anyone reads have scrolled
past. Since 6.3.7\-1 the client loads the font itself, early, and the boxes are
gone.
.IP "" 4
Note that console\-setup still loads its own font later, over the top. This
directive decides what the early boot looks like, not what the console settles
on; for both, configure console\-setup in the image.
.SH "CLIENT IDENTITY"
.PP
.IP "\fBHOSTNAME\fP" 4
The client's name, set outright. Leave it unset and the name is built from
\fBHOSTNAME_BASE\fR and \fBHOSTNAME_EXTRA\fR instead, which is what a fleet
wants: one lts.conf, a different name per machine.
.IP "\fBHOSTNAME_BASE\fP" 4
The fixed part, \fBltsp\fR by default.
.IP "\fBHOSTNAME_EXTRA\fP" 4
What distinguishes one client from the next. \fBip\fR (the default) appends the
host part of the address, so 192.168.67.20 in a /24 becomes \fBltsp20\fR;
\fBmac\fR appends the MAC with dashes, which survives a changed address but is
unreadable across a room. A name without either is the same on every client,
which is fine for one machine on a bench and useless in a lab \(em the greeter
shows this name, and it is the first thing anyone reads off a screen when
reporting a fault.
.IP "" 4
Both forms need \fBiproute2\fR in the image; \fIltsp\-client\-core\fR depends
on it since 6.3.3\-1. An older image without it produces the bare base name
with nothing appended, and silently \(em there is no error, just every client
called \fBltsp\fR.
.IP "\fBLDM_CLOCK_FORMAT\fP" 4
The date and time in the greeter's status line, as an \fBstrftime\fR(3) format
that may contain Pango markup. The default is \fB"%Y\-%m\-%d, <b>%H:%M</b>"\fR.
For the German convention:
.IP "" 4
\fBLDM_CLOCK_FORMAT="%d.%m.%Y, <b>%H:%M</b>"\fR
.IP "" 4
Deliberately not \fB%x\fR, which is the locale's date format and therefore only
as good as the locale the image happens to carry \(em a stock build is en_US,
and \fB%x\fR then shows 09/17/2026 to a room in Germany. Worse than foreign, it
is ambiguous: 09/10 is two different days depending on the reader.
.SH "GRAPHICS"
.PP
.IP "\fBX_COLOR_DEPTH\fP, \fBX_SMART_COLOR_DEPTH\fP" 4
LTSP defaults thin clients to 16 bits per pixel to save bandwidth. Modern KMS
drivers reject that outright \(em Xorg logs \fBfailed to add fb \-22\fR and the
server exits \(em so the 16\-bit default now applies only where there is no
\fI/dev/dri/card0\fR. On anything with a KMS device, leave both unset and let
Xorg pick 24 or 32.
.IP "\fBCONFIGURE_X\fP" 4
Whether to write an \fIxorg.conf\fR at all. Off by default, and rightly: a
modern Xorg configures itself better than a generated file can, and several
directives below only take effect because setting them turns this on
implicitly.
.IP "\fBCONFIGURE_X_COMMAND\fP, \fBX_CONF\fP" 4
The command that generates the file, and where it is written.
\fBXF86CONFIG_FILE\fR is the LTSP 4 name for the latter.
.IP "\fBXSERVER\fP" 4
The X server or driver to use. \fBauto\fR, the default, lets Xorg decide.
Naming a driver is a way to work around a broken autodetection and a way to
create one.
.IP "\fBX_HORZSYNC\fP, \fBX_VERTREFRESH\fP" 4
The monitor's sync ranges, for a monitor that cannot be asked. Only reached
when an \fIxorg.conf\fR is written. \fBX_HORIZSYNC\fR is accepted as a
spelling variant for LTSP 4 configurations.
.IP "\fBMODELINES\fP" 4
Extra modelines for the same case.
.IP "\fBX_VIDEO_RAM\fP" 4
Video memory in kilobytes, for hardware that misreports it.
\fBX_VIDEORAM\fR is the LTSP 4 spelling.
.IP "\fBX_RAMPERC\fP" 4
The percentage of free memory the X server may use before the screen gives up
rather than swapping the client to a standstill. 100 by default, which means
no limit.
.IP "\fBX_VIRTUAL\fP, \fBX_VIRTUAL_AUTO\fP" 4
A virtual screen larger than the monitor, given outright or computed from the
\fBXRANDR_MODE_\fP\fIn\fP entries. Needed for a multi\-head client, because the
virtual size has to cover every head and Xorg fixes it at startup.
.IP "\fBX_OPTION_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Extra \fBOption\fR lines for the device section, written in order.
.IP "\fBX_MONITOR_OPTION_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
The same for the monitor section.
.IP "\fBX_MOUSE_DEVICE\fP, \fBX_MOUSE_PROTOCOL\fP, \fBX_MOUSE_EMULATE3BTN\fP" 4
A mouse that is not on the usual device nodes: which device, which protocol
(\fBauto\fR by default) and whether two buttons should stand in for three.
Serial mice, in other words.
.IP "\fBUSE_TOUCH\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_DEVICE\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_DRIVER\fP" 4
A touchscreen: whether to configure one, on which device, with which driver.
.IP "\fBX_TOUCH_MINX\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_MAXX\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_MINY\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_MAXY\fP" 4
Its calibration.
.IP "\fBX_TOUCH_RPTDELAY\fP, \fBX_TOUCH_UNDELAY\fP" 4
Its report and untouch delays.
.IP "\fBX_NUMLOCK\fP" 4
Whether Num Lock is on when the session starts. Needs \fBnumlockx\fR in the
image; without it the directive does nothing, silently.
.IP "\fBX_BLANKING\fP" 4
Minutes before the screen blanks, or \fB0\fR to disable blanking and DPMS
together.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_DISABLE\fP" 4
Turns the whole xrandr stage off, for hardware where it does more harm than
good.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_OUTPUT\fP, \fBXRANDR_MODE\fP, \fBXRANDR_RATE\fP" 4
A single display: which output, which mode, which refresh rate.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_ROTATE\fP, \fBXRANDR_REFLECT\fP, \fBXRANDR_ORIENTATION\fP" 4
Rotation, mirroring and orientation of that display.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_DPI\fP, \fBXRANDR_SIZE\fP" 4
Its resolution in dots per inch and its physical size.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_NEWMODE\fP" 4
A mode to define first, for a monitor whose EDID does not offer the one
wanted.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_OUTPUT_\fP\fIn\fP and friends" 4
Every directive in this group also exists with a trailing index \(em
\fBXRANDR_OUTPUT_0\fR, \fBXRANDR_MODE_0\fR, \fBXRANDR_RATE_0\fR and so on \(em
for a client with several displays. Index 0 is the first head.
.IP "\fBX_MODE_0\fP" 4
An older spelling of \fBXRANDR_NEWMODE_0\fR, still honoured.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_AUTO_MULTIHEAD\fP" 4
Arranges the connected outputs left to right without naming them, which covers
most two\-head clients.
.IP "\fBXRANDR_ARGS\fP, \fBXRANDR_COMMAND_\fP\fIn\fP" 4
Arguments appended to every xrandr call, and whole commands to run afterwards
when the directives above cannot express what is wanted.
.SH "CLIENT TYPE"
.PP
.IP "\fBLTSP_FATCLIENT\fP" 4
Whether the session runs on the client rather than on a server. Normally not
set by hand: it is derived from the image. If the image contains a local
desktop session \(em any \fI/usr/share/xsessions/*.desktop\fR \(em the client is
fat, otherwise thin. That makes the variant a property of the image, so thin
and fat fleets are two images rather than two configurations, and per\-client
selection becomes a question of which image a client boots.
.IP "" 4
A consequence worth knowing: adding a desktop package to a thin image to try
something out silently turns it into a fat image at the next boot.
.IP "\fBFAT_RAM_THRESHOLD\fP" 4
Megabytes of RAM below which a client stays thin even on a fat image.
Defaults to 400.
.SH "SERVICES AND UPDATES"
.PP
.IP "\fBKEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES\fP" 4
Services the thin\-client cleanup would otherwise remove from the running
client, space separated. \fB"ssh"\fR is useful while building an image and has
no place in a production one.
.IP "" 4
Note that \fBsystemd\-logind\fR is no longer removed at all, whatever this is
set to. Xorg 21 needs a logind session to obtain its DRM and input file
descriptors; without it the X server exits immediately and is respawned
forever, which presents as a text console rather than as an error.
.IP "\fBLDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE\fP" 4
Whether ldm reboots the client when it finds a newer image on the server. Off
by default. Reasonable for an unattended fleet, startling for anyone sitting
in front of one \(em the greeter simply disappears mid\-login.
.IP "\fBRM_SYSTEM_SERVICES\fP" 4
Services to remove from the running client, on top of the built\-in list. The
built\-in list covers what a diskless client has no use for \(em a mail
transport, a DHCP server, an automatic updater. Removal happens in RAM; the
image is untouched.
.IP "\fBRM_THIN_SYSTEM_SERVICES\fP" 4
The same, applied only to thin clients. Fat clients keep these, because they
run a real desktop and need the machinery behind it.
.IP "\fBRM_LOW_RAM_SERVICES\fP, \fBRM_RAM_THRESHOLD\fP" 4
A further list, applied only below \fBRM_RAM_THRESHOLD\fR megabytes of RAM
(200 by default). For hardware where cron and its neighbours are the
difference between a session and a swap storm.
.IP "\fBRM_SESSION_SERVICES\fP, \fBKEEP_SESSION_SERVICES\fP" 4
The same idea one level up: autostart entries removed from the desktop
session, and the exceptions to that.
.IP "\fBDISABLE_GETTYS\fP" 4
Whether to remove the text logins on terminals 2 to 6. True by default. Set it
false to keep a console on a machine being worked on \(em and remember that a
console on a thin client is a root prompt.
.IP "\fBDEFAULT_DISPLAY_MANAGER\fP" 4
What goes into \fI/etc/X11/default\-display\-manager\fR, \fI/usr/sbin/ldm\fR
unless set. An empty value leaves the file alone, which is what a fat client
running its own display manager wants.
.SH "SOUND"
.PP
.IP "\fBSOUND\fP" 4
Whether sound is available in the session. Derived rather than configured:
true on a thin client when a sound daemon is present, false on a fat client,
which plays its own audio locally and needs no forwarding.
.IP "\fBSOUND_DAEMON\fP" 4
Which daemon does it \(em \fBpulse\fR when PulseAudio is in the image. Also
derived, and forced off for fat clients.
.SH "LOCAL DEVICES AND APPLICATIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fBLOCALDEV\fP" 4
Whether USB sticks and other media plugged into the client appear inside the
session. Derived: true when \fBltspfsd\fR is in the image, off for fat
clients, which mount their own media directly.
.IP "\fBLOCAL_STORAGE\fP" 4
The LTSP 4 name for the same thing, honoured for old configurations.
.IP "\fBLOCAL_APPS\fP" 4
Whether applications may run on the client instead of the server, with the
user's home directory mounted over sshfs. Derived: true when \fBsshfs\fR is in
the image. The sshfs mount is skipped when the home directory already arrives
another way, for example over NFS on a fat client.
.IP "\fBSCANNER\fP" 4
Whether to start \fBsaned\fR on the client, so a scanner attached to it can be
reached from the session.
.SH "FILESYSTEMS AND HOME DIRECTORIES"
.PP
.IP "\fBCONFIGURE_FSTAB\fP" 4
Whether LTSP writes the client's \fI/etc/fstab\fR at boot. True unless set
otherwise; false leaves whatever the image carries.
.IP "\fBFSTAB_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Additional \fIfstab\fR lines, written in the order of their numbers. The whole
line, exactly as it would appear in the file.
.IP "\fBNFS_HOME\fP" 4
The home directories, mounted over NFS. Either \fIserver:/path\fR or just
\fI/path\fR, in which case the boot server is used. The mount point is created
if it does not exist. This is how a fat client gets real home directories
instead of writing into RAM that vanishes at logout.
.IP "\fBNFS_HOME_OPTIONS\fP" 4
Mount options for it, \fBdefaults\fR unless set.
.IP "\fBOVERLAY_DIR\fP" 4
A directory in the image whose contents are unpacked over \fI/\fR at boot,
\fI/etc/ltsp/overlay\-dir\fR by default. The way to place a handful of files on
every client without a hook script.
.SH "SWAP"
.PP
.IP "\fBUSE_LOCAL_SWAP\fP" 4
Whether to use swap partitions found on a local disk. Off by default, because
a thin client is not supposed to touch the disk it boots past.
.IP "\fBNBD_SWAP\fP" 4
Whether to swap over the network to the server. Switched on automatically
below \fBNBD_SWAP_THRESHOLD\fR megabytes of RAM when no local swap was found.
.IP "\fBNBD_SWAP_THRESHOLD\fP" 4
That threshold: 800 MB for a thin client, 2100 for a fat one.
.IP "\fBNBD_SWAP_HOST\fP" 4
Which server serves it, the boot server unless set.
.IP "\fBENCRYPT_SWAP\fP" 4
Whether swap is encrypted. Derived: true when \fBcryptsetup\fR is in the
image. Worth leaving on \(em network swap carries whatever was in the session's
memory across the wire.
.SH "TIME"
.PP
.IP "\fBTIMEZONE\fP" 4
The client's time zone, as a zoneinfo name such as \fBEurope/Berlin\fR.
Without it the client displays UTC, which looks like a clock that is wrong
rather than a clock in another zone \(em the greeter shows it, and so does
every log line written on the client.
.IP "\fBTIMESERVER\fP" 4
An NTP server to set the clock from at boot, or \fBauto\fR for the boot
server. Needs \fBntpdate\fR in the image; where systemd's time
synchronisation already runs, this is unnecessary.
.IP "\fBHWCLOCK\fP" 4
Whether the hardware clock is written back as \fButc\fR (the default) or
\fBlocal\fR.
.SH "NETWORK AND NAMES"
.PP
.IP "\fBSERVER\fP" 4
The boot server, used as the default for nearly everything else here \(em
lts.conf is fetched from it, swap and syslog go to it, and it is the fallback
session server. Normally derived from how the client booted.
.IP "\fBSERVER_NAME\fP" 4
The name that server is given in the client's \fI/etc/hosts\fR, \fBserver\fR
by default.
.IP "\fBDNS_SERVER\fP" 4
Nameservers for the client, space separated. Unset, the client uses what DHCP
gave it, then tries the boot server and the gateway in turn.
.IP "\fBSEARCH_DOMAIN\fP, \fBDNSDOMAIN\fP" 4
The DNS search domain, and the domain appended to the client's own name.
.IP "\fBHOSTS_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Additional \fI/etc/hosts\fR lines, in the order of their numbers.
.IP "\fBNET_DEVICE_METHOD\fP" 4
How \fI/etc/network/interfaces\fR describes the boot interface, \fBmanual\fR
by default \(em the address is already configured by the time that file
matters, and anything else invites a second configuration of the interface the
root filesystem is arriving over.
.IP "\fBSYSLOG_HOST\fP" 4
A host to send the client's log to. On a thin client \fI/var/log\fR is in RAM
and dies with the client, so this is the only way to read afterwards what
happened during a boot that failed.
.SH "LOCAL KERNEL UPDATES"
.PP
A client that boots its kernel from a local disk rather than over the
network needs that copy refreshed when the image gets a new one.
.IP "\fBKERNEL_DEVICE\fP" 4
The partition holding it, for example
\fB/dev/disk/by\-label/LTSP\fR. Nothing happens unless this is set and
the device exists. It can also come from the kernel command line as
\fBltsp.kernel_device=\fR.
.IP "\fBKERNEL_NAME\fP, \fBINITRD_NAME\fP" 4
What the kernel and initrd are called there, \fBvmlinuz\fR and
\fBinitrd.img\fR unless set.
.SH "RUNNING THINGS AT BOOT"
.PP
.IP "\fBMODULE_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Kernel modules to load, with their parameters, in the order of their numbers.
The whole \fBmodprobe\fR argument list, not only the name.
.IP "\fBINIT_COMMAND_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Shell commands run during the client's startup, in the order of their numbers.
They are sourced, not executed, so they see and can change the LTSP variables.
.IP "\fBRCFILE_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Lines appended to \fI/etc/rc.local\fR, which runs later than
\fBINIT_COMMAND_\fP\fInn\fP \(em after the services are up rather than while
the client is still assembling itself.
.IP "\fBCRONTAB_\fP\fInn\fP" 4
Lines added to the client's crontab. Full crontab syntax including the user
field.
.IP "\fBSHUTDOWN_TIME\fP" 4
A time of day, \fBHH:MM\fR, at which the client switches itself off. A client
with a session open is not cut off mid\-work: it is marked to shut down when
that session ends.
.SH "THE CONFIGURATION FILE ITSELF"
.PP
.IP "\fBLTSCONF\fP" 4
The file name fetched over TFTP, \fBlts.conf\fR unless set. The directory
comes from the boot path \(em see FILES.
.IP "\fBPROCESS_LTS_CONF\fP" 4
Whether lts.conf is read at all. Off means the image's built\-in defaults
stand, whatever the server offers.
.IP "" 4
Not everything LTSP reads lives here. \fBltsp\-update\-kernels\fR(8)
takes its settings \(em \fBBOOT_METHODS\fR, the \fBCMDLINE_\fR series,
\fBPXELINUX_DEFAULT\fR and the rest of the boot\-menu directives \(em from
\fI/etc/ltsp/update\-kernels.conf\fR in the image, not from lts.conf.
.SH "FILES"
.PP
.IP "\fI/etc/ltsp/lts.conf\fP" 4
The commented example, on the server.
.IP "\fI/etc/lts.conf\fP" 4
Where the client reads it from \(em getltscfg(1) opens this path and no other.
.IP "" 4
It is not necessarily the copy that was in the image.
\fIinit\-ltsp.d/05\-getltsconffile\fR fetches lts.conf over TFTP from the boot
server early in the client's startup and overwrites this file when the
transfer returns something non\-empty, so a client can be reconfigured without
rebuilding the image. That also means the contents arrive over an
unauthenticated channel, which is worth knowing before putting anything
security\-relevant in them \(em see SSH HOST KEYS.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBgetltscfg\fP(1),
\fBinit\-ltsp\fP(1),
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-app\fP(8),
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-root\fP(8),
\fBltsp\-update\-sshkeys\fP(8),
the lab HOWTO under /usr/share/doc/ltsp\-server.
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
Part of the \fBltsp\-server\fP package; the latest version is available from
\fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
